.TH VOICETEST 1
.SH NAME
voicetest \- check the speech device
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B voicetest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Voicetest
checks
.IR voice (3).
It prints
.B PASS
or a
.B FAIL
line per problem.
It needs no display and no window manager.
.SH "WHAT IT ESTABLISHES"
Nothing in this system can hear, so this does not claim the speech is
intelligible.
It claims what can be established from the samples themselves.
.PP
That writing phonemes makes a sound: not merely that samples came back, since
silence is samples too, but that a tenth of them or more carry signal.
And that a symbol the device does not know is skipped rather than silencing the
whole utterance.
.PP
That
.B speed
does what it says.
Twice the speed must be half the samples, within a tenth.
.SS "Pitch, by correlating the sound with itself"
This is the check worth having.
Everything else about the device could be right while the voice source ran at
one fixed rate, and the result would still be speech-shaped sound of the
correct length \(em it would just always be the same voice.
.PP
A voiced source repeating every
.I P
samples makes a waveform that resembles itself
.I P
samples later.
At 16000 Hz, 100 Hz is 160 samples and 200 Hz is 80.
So the test is not whether the number in
.B ctl
changed; it is whether the sound repeats at the period that number implies.
.PP
At
.B "pitch 100"
the waveform correlates 0.88 with itself at a lag of 160 samples and
.B \-0.49
at 80 \(em anti-correlated at half a period, which is what a periodic wave
does.
At
.B "pitch 200"
both lags are high, 0.86 and 0.86, because 80 samples is now one period and 160
is two.
.PP
Both controls have been seen to fail.
Fixing the source at 100 Hz regardless of
.B ctl
fails the pitch check by name; ignoring
.B speed
reports 4800 samples where 2400 were wanted.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/voicetest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR voice (3),
.IR speak (1),
.IR runtests (1)
.SH BUGS
It does not check that any particular phoneme sounds like that phoneme.
The formant targets are verified once, by hand, by taking the spectrum of a
held vowel and seeing the peaks land near the table \(em that is written up in
.IR voice (3)
and is not repeated here, because a discrete Fourier transform in Limbo over
enough samples to be meaningful is slower than the rest of this test together.
.PP
The
.B announce
levels are only checked to be accepted and reported back.
What each one lets through needs a window system and a focus to move, so it is
checked by hand under
.IR wm (1).
